lesson.body:
Faced with a large, unfamiliar problem, most people freeze or feel overwhelmed. Decomposition is the antidote: it means splitting a big problem into smaller pieces, each of which is simple enough to solve on its own. This is one of the four strategies of computational thinking, and it is arguably the most practical one, because almost no real program is written as a single, giant block of instructions. Instead, programs are built as collections of small, manageable pieces that work together, the way a construction crew does not build an entire house in one continuous motion but frames the walls, runs the wiring, and finishes the plumbing as separate, sequenced jobs.

Suppose you need to write a program that reads a list of test scores and prints a short report showing the average, the highest score, and whether the class passed overall. Trying to write this as one long, tangled procedure invites mistakes, since a single misplaced line can silently corrupt every calculation that follows it. Decomposition suggests separating the problem into distinct subtasks: computing an average, finding the highest value, and formatting a report. Each subtask becomes its own function, which connects directly to abstraction, since each function hides its own internal logic behind a clear name that the rest of the program can simply trust.

In Python, this might look like:

def get_average(scores):
    return sum(scores) / len(scores)
def get_highest(scores):
    return max(scores)
def print_report(scores):
    average = get_average(scores)
    highest = get_highest(scores)
    print("Average:", average)
    print("Highest:", highest)
    print("Class passed:", average >= 70)

Trace this with scores = [65, 82, 91, 70]. get_average adds the four scores to get 308, then divides by 4 to get 77.0. get_highest scans the list and returns 91. print_report then prints the average, the highest, and evaluates average >= 70, which is True, so it reports the class passed. Notice that each function can be tested and understood completely on its own. If get_average had a bug, you could test it by itself with a small list like [10, 20] and confirm it returns 15.0, without needing to worry about get_highest or print_report at all, since a bug isolated to one small function is far easier to locate than a bug hidden somewhere inside one enormous block of code.

This independence is the real payoff of decomposition. Large software projects, built by teams of hundreds of programmers, rely on the same idea at a much bigger scale: different people or teams own different pieces, each with a clear interface, and the pieces are combined at the end, often without any single person understanding every subtask in full detail. Decomposition alone, however, only tells you how to split a problem. Deciding how to split it well, and noticing that some of those smaller pieces look similar to problems you have already solved, is where the next strategy, pattern recognition, comes in.
